# What never goes into the repository.
#
# Cold Start step 4.4. Two different jobs are mixed in here on purpose and it is
# worth knowing which is which:
#
#   * the machine's litter (caches, editor state) — nobody minds if this leaks,
#     it is just noise in every diff;
#   * YOUR DATA — where a mistake is not noise. A dataset committed once is in the
#     history for good, and removing it means rewriting every commit after it. If
#     the repository is public by then, it has already been cloned.
#
# The second job is why the last section exists and why it is yours to fill in.
# `git check-ignore -v <path>` tells you which line here is responsible for a file
# being ignored, and is the fastest way to find out why something is not showing up.

# ---- the machine's litter ----
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*~
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.Rhistory
.Rproj.user/
node_modules/
.venv/
venv/
env/
.env
*.log

# ---- credentials, which are never a mistake worth making twice ----
*.pem
*.key
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.*
credentials.*

# ---- data, by extension ----
# Broad on purpose. It is far cheaper to force-add one file you meant to commit
# (`git add -f small-example.csv`) than to discover a 4 GB file in your history.
*.csv
*.tsv
*.parquet
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.nc
*.mat
*.npy
*.npz
*.sav
*.dta
*.rds
*.RData
*.dcm
*.nii
*.nii.gz
*.tif
*.tiff
*.bam
*.fastq
*.fastq.gz

# ---- data, by location ----
# Whole folders, so a file type nobody anticipated is still covered. The names are
# guesses at what yours are called; the real one is whatever `data` says in
# docs/SETUP.md, and if that folder lives inside this project it belongs here too.
data/
raw/
raw_data/
scratch/
tmp/
output/
outputs/
results/
figures/

# ---- yours ----
# Add the folder `data` names in docs/SETUP.md if it is inside this project, and
# anything else this list has not anticipated. When you add a line, say why in a
# comment — a bare pattern in six months is a thing nobody dares delete.
